810/D15/0/code.py:64:#   partition: plus colors are unique and marker colors avoid them.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/53fb4810/D15/0/code.py:178:    - Small marker patches touch one arm of each plus, and marker colors avoid every plus color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a395ee82/3/code.py:20:#      1. All singleton pixels share one marker color O, so color no longer identifies the origin.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a395ee82/3/code.py:231:    - Elsewhere there is a uniform-spacing lattice of singleton pixels, and every singleton uses the same marker color O.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/2/code.py:210:    - A square colored grid contains one solid rectangular marker color hiding the answer patch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e376de54/34/code.py:189:    assert _EXT_MARK != bg and _TRAIL_COLOR != bg, \"marker colors must differ from background\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a395ee82/11/code.py:97:#   singletons share one marker color. The generator constructs that directly.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/16/code.py:34:#   1. marker color is fixed to 8 and forms one solid rectangle;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6ffbe589/12/code.py:212:    - Read the marker color, if any, from the top-left, top-right, and bottom-right cue pads.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/9/code.py:38:#    Each sample uses only 4-5 non-marker colors even though the global palette is larger.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/9/code.py:160:    - A square grid of side 10-15 containing one solid rectangular marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/26/code.py:11:#      We fix 4 reusable marker colors: 2=up, 1=right, 7=down, 8=left. The cue pixel may\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/26/code.py:25:#      2-3 shapes. Shape colors come from {3,4,6}; marker colors come from {1,2,7,8}.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/26/code.py:34:#      object. That breaks once marker colors are globally reusable, so marker colors must\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/26/code.py:42:#      encoded-direction cues, so the render pass first erases marker colors from the base.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/26/code.py:53:#    - Humans can read two repeated signals at once: the same marker color always produces\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/26/code.py:279:    Connect same-color vertices as striped trails, where a neighboring marker color encodes the true shadow direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/26/code.py:283:    - Shape vertices use non-marker colors and appear as 2+ points that can be ordered into\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/26/code.py:292:    - Copy each edge in the marker color's encoded direction, not the cue's geometric side,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D2/2/code.py:21:#    - Matching is done by marker color, not by the motif body's color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D2/2/code.py:28:#      body color, and several distractor marker colors within the 10-color palette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D2/2/code.py:29:#    - Matching marker colors repeat exactly twice in the input: once above one block\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D2/2/code.py:30:#      and once above one true motif. Distractor marker colors appear only above motifs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D2/2/code.py:46:#      marker colors, the new rule is visible and cannot be mistaken for body-color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D2/2/code.py:61:#    - Every block-marker color appears on exactly one true motif marker.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D2/2/code.py:124:    # STEP 2) Identify 3x3 motif bodies and index them by their marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/10/code.py:223:    - Detect the nested outer-shell/inner-core cross pair and infer the reflected strip start while ignoring both marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/10/code.py:228:    The two marker colors are two occlusion layers over the same symmetric texture. Some inner-cross cells cannot trust the raw\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/21/code.py:24:#      2. restore that outer ring while treating both marker colors as hidden.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/21/code.py:230:    - The unique successful color on the raw input is the outer layer; recover that ring while treating both marker colors as\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/19/code.py:25:#      plausible markers by shape, but only the real marker color is responsible\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/20/code.py:21:#      seed cross-span <= 2, and 1-4 marker colors per seed.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/4/code.py:55:#      1. there is exactly one solid rectangular marker color;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/4/code.py:194:    - A square colored board contains one solid rectangular marker color covering a patch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e87109e9/D3/3/code.py:36:#      sparse local marker color inside the main board.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e87109e9/D3/3/code.py:296:    assert bg != MARKER, \"marker color cannot be the board background\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/18/code.py:33:#    - Only one extra symbol is introduced: marker color 8. It always means the waterline and is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D8/1/code.py:47:    - Across 3-6 blocks per sample, the same special marker color repeatedly indicates different carve offsets.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D8/1/code.py:52:    - The marker color is reserved for anchors only, avoiding mixed semantics elsewhere.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D8/1/code.py:392:    \"\"\"Pick destination colors, distractor colors, and one reserved marker color.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/5/code.py:30:#    - Small trace: suppose the marker color is 6, the centered decoy uses color 8, and two tiny\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/13/code.py:162:    - A square colored grid contains one solid rectangular marker, but the marker color is not fixed across examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/13/code.py:163:    - The marker color is the unique most frequent color in the whole input.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/13/code.py:167:    - Count color frequencies and select the unique highest-frequency color as the marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/23/code.py:22:#    Small 10-15 grids and only 3-4 non-marker colors simplify the visual load,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/23/code.py:29:#      fixed marker color 8 directly and only checks that its footprint is a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/23/code.py:164:      or vertical monochrome stripes with only 3-4 non-marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a395ee82/19/code.py:61:    - Separate singleton pixels form a uniform-spacing lattice. Exactly one singleton uses a dedicated marker color `M`.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/21/code.py:80:#    visible count of distinct marker colors, and the cycle is supported by\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/0934a4d8/22/code.py:138:    - A 30x30 square grid with one unique solid rectangular marker color covering a missing patch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D1/1/code.py:118:    - Several 5x5 non-bg blocks whose 1-cell border is a uniform marker color and whose inner 3x3 is a different uniform fill color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58f5dbd5/D1/1/code.py:119:    - Elsewhere, one small motif sprite per border-marker color, plus optional distractor motifs of unrelated colors.\n/home/lix