     Generator always includes marker colors 2 and 3 in at least one real overlap because that pair makes\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/21/code.py:351:    Key insight: The marker color still selects the sprite, but the marker position controls orientation and the anchor-color ordering controls which rotated stamp stays visible in overlap zones.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/27/code.py:58:#    - Only one motion axis per assembly, one marker color, and rectangular boxes are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/35/code.py:241:    - Black background with marker colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8, but each logical marker is now an\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3e6067c3/25/code.py:30:# - All control runs use one dedicated marker color absent from frame paints, so color matching cannot\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/32/code.py:175:    - The four shapes are still identified by marker color, but the color transformation disappears: samples and generated instances now share the same color system, and only the label pixel is suppressed.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:19:      item 3, then the cycle repeats. This assignment ignores marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:31:        3. painting still happens by marker color, so the color-2 blue ring paints before the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:41:    - The generator favors 3 or 4 marker colors so several batch levels are visible. It also makes\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:42:      the row width 5 or 6, not a multiple of the 4-step cycle, so a single marker color appears\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:45:    - Original assumption: marker color directly selects one glyph entry from GLYPHS.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:52:      Broken here: a marker color no longer implies one glyph, so label detection must match\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:53:      against any source glyph drawn at (-4,-4), independent of marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:71:    - Human-solvable clue for D2/2: the same marker color appears in multiple scan phases and thus\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:76:    - main() assumes every real marker color is one of {2,3,5,8}; generator samples only those.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/17/code.py:277:    - Marker color no longer tells you which glyph to draw; position in the global scan order does that, while marker color only decides when that preassigned glyph gets to overwrite others.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a47bf94d/D2/2/code.py:306:    Route colored 3x3 tokens through pipe networks whose crossings are controlled by marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a47bf94d/D2/2/code.py:322:    - The wire layout is no longer solved by plain greedy tracing: the marker color at each crossing\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/5/code.py:217:    - Read the legend first to convert every destination marker color into the matching source body color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/5/code.py:221:    Key insight: The marker color is only a code; the legend reveals the real template key, and that key is the sprite's majority color rather than its anchor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/15/code.py:256:    - Convert each real marker color into the same fixed glyph mapping as the base task: 2->yellow frame, 3->blue ring, 5->magenta diagonal blocks, 8->orange X.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/15/code.py:261:    - The dictionary from marker color to 4x4 shape is unchanged, but the spatial cue has changed twice: markers must be found one by one because they are randomly scattered, and each glyph must be painted with toroidal coordinates rather than rectangular clipping.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/28/code.py:22:#        original task: marker color = A, align local A at (0,0) to the marker.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/28/code.py:23:#        fused variant: marker color = B, align local center C at (1,1) to the marker.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/28/code.py:44:#    - The marker color now matches the repeated body color visible in source sprites.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/28/code.py:45:#    - The marker position in the output becomes the center decoration color, not the input marker color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/28/code.py:95:    - For every destination marker, select the sprite whose body color matches the marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/20/code.py:79:#      of one pre-drawn 4x4 glyph and a matching label marker at (+4, +4). The marker color still\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/34/code.py:91:   - main() assumes every destination marker color matches some source anchor color; generator\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/34/code.py:527:    - Match each destination marker color to the source sprite with the same anchor color, clear only the destination interior, and stamp those sprites there with anchor alignment.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/16/code.py:87:#    Only two marker colors are used, and only one nesting layer is added. No object stacks on top of another object's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/16/code.py:98:#    and right. `generate_input()` guarantees that shape, guarantees both marker colors are present, and keeps colors 1/4\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/9/code.py:9:#      can itself be another marker color. We concretize \"corner-triggered propagation\" to that single corner,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/9/code.py:29:#      agree on marker color; the generator enforces this by rejecting conflicting samples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/9/code.py:52:#      marker colors through propagation; the generator checks both conditions before returning a sample.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/9/code.py:232:                    raise ValueError(\"conflicting discovered marker colors\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/9/code.py:239:                raise ValueError(\"conflicting next-wave marker colors\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/9/code.py:315:    - Unlike the original task, some visible sample/template corners can themselves use marker colors because those corners explain the propagation rule.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/9/code.py:320:    - After each wave, if a placed template's lower-right corner cell is another marker color, treat that corner as a fresh marker for the next wave.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/13/code.py:20:#        old tri-color intuition: marker color might try to match a singleton accent\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/13/code.py:21:#        new combo rule: marker color matches the center cell, and stamping aligns that center cell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/13/code.py:29:#      the marker color is no longer an accent color, and the paste offset is no longer measured\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/13/code.py:69:#    - Every sprite's center color is unique across types and appears as a destination marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/31f7f899/14/code.py:26:#   just outside the frame wall, so the solver must match source bar color to hole marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/28/code.py:71:  panel and only in the special marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/10/code.py:121:    - For each marker color, locate its legend label and read the 4x4 sample immediately above-left of that label.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/23/code.py:19:#     - Preserve color matching: each singleton marker color still selects one sprite type.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/23/code.py:32:#       - For each marker color, find the matching source sprite.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/23/code.py:52:#         same marker color would stamp that bottom-right-anchor source view directly, with no reorientation.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/34/code.py:232:    - Elsewhere, small 3×3 motif sprites are bicolored: one color matches a block and the other is a shared marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/34/code.py:237:    - Only blocks whose packed-grid position has even row+column parity use their motif; those selected blocks repaint the entire motif footprint to the marker color at the decoded offset, while the other blocks stay solid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/34/code.py:425:    \"\"\"Pick 1-2 occupied cells that visibly carry the marker color in the input motif.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/34/code.py:438:    \"\"\"Pick block and distractor primary colors, reserving the shared marker color.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/34/code.py:540:    \"\"\"Paint the 2x2 alignment legend using the shared marker color.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D5/1/code.py:20:#       that square is recolored with a visible marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D5/1/code.py:55:#       the target anchor's marker colors either already match the transformed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D5/1/code.py:73:#       The dedicated marker color is just a visible structural cue.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/21/code.py:78:#    - Example with marker color 2 (yellow frame) at top-lefts (1,1) and (1,3):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/21/code.py:92:#      blocks.  Different marker colors can still appear, but only in regions\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/19/code.py:25:#     - The outer lane of a band is constant marker color on data slots; the inner lane uses marker color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/19/code.py:29:#     - Original: a marker color directly implied one fixed mask and one fixed output color, and an optional\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/19/code.py:31:#     - Fusion: a marker color is only a key. Example: if a top-edge band has outer row = all 2 on data slots\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/19/code.py:56:#     - The constant outer lane announces the controlling marker color, while the inner lane makes the output\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/19/code.py:251:    - In each band, the outer lane is the marker color and the inner lane is a separator-grouped 16-cell flattening of the 4x4 glyph, using marker color for empty cells and glyph color for filled cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/6/code.py:13:#      A destination marker colored 7 selects that sprite, but the pasted sprite is translated so the lone 2 lands\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/6/code.py:44:#      2. every destination marker color belongs to the source body-color set and to none of the anchor colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/6/code.py:91:    - Match each destination singleton to the source sprite whose body color equals the marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/9/code.py:43:# - To keep the color-pairing rule readable, marker colors come from a small fixed palette and are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/9/code.py:62:#   cannot require an existing target. The generator guarantees only one marker color is adjacent to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/9/code.py:94:# - Assumption: every container has exactly one adjacent marker color touching its border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a25697e4/5/code.py:26:#      third marker color. The solver therefore isolates the smallest color-count as the marker\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a25697e4/5/code.py:75:      swap those two colors while leaving the marker color unchanged.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/30/code.py:83:#    - Every destination marker color appears as a source anchor color, repeated anchors span multiple\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/4/code.py:200:        assert len(matches) == 1, f\"expected exactly one legend for marker color {marker_color}, got {len(matches)}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/4/code.py:217:    - The legend covers all four marker colors, but its order, sample colors, and 4x4 masks can change from puzzle to puzzle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/4/code.py:220:    - For each label pixel, read the 4x4 patch immediately above-left of it to learn both the output color and the glyph mask owned by that marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a251c730/14/code.py:63:#       generator preserves that and ensures each marker color maps to exactly one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/28/code.py:237:    - The lower band contains the real marker lattice whose colors reuse the same six marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/28/code.py:240:    - For each legend entry, the label marker color names the glyph drawn immediately up-left of it; together, the six entries define the per-puzzle marker-to-glyph mapping.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/28/code.py:255:    assert present_marker_colors == set(MARKER_COLORS), \"expected all 6 marker colors to appear\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a25697e4/23/code.py:27:#   new solver uses \"one non-marker color count + 1 == cavity size\". The original reflection-based\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/13/code.py:217:    - Shape and color have been decoupled: the marker color still names the geometry, while the displayed color is a uniform arithmetic offset, causing color 8 markers to disappear into the black background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/23/code.py:101:#       legend colors {1,4,6,7} remain disjoint from marker colors {2,3,5,8}.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/29/code.py:244:    - Interior movable objects are separated multi-color sprites, plus occasional singleton distractors, whose rare marker color identifies a border symbol.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/29/code.py:248:    - Pair each movable object with the border segment of the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:43:#    - Left legend band: exactly four entries are shown, one per marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:57:#    - exactly four marker colors and four glyph colors are reused, not expanded;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:58:#    - the body always contains all four marker colors, each at least twice, so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:74:#    - Because the legend is complete, `main()` assumes each marker color has\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:109:#    `main()` assumes every real marker color has exactly one legend label, the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:177:        assert len(matches) == 1, f\"marker color {marker_color} must have exactly one legend label\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:205:    - The body contains single-pixel markers in colors 2, 3, 5, and 8; the same marker colors also\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:209:    - For each marker color, read the 4x4 monochrome sample immediately above-left of its legend label.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:211:      marker color; also confirm that the four learned masks are exactly the four quarter-turn rotations\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/22/code.py:219:      each marker color and which rotation of the puzzle-specific base glyph that marker should use.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/18/code.py:9:#    - Seed task: a border anchor color matches a sprite's rare marker color; top/bottom anchors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/18/code.py:15:#        3) after a sprite moves away, repaint its original 4-neighbor outline in marker color and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/18/code.py:40:#    - Movement assumes one anchor per marker color; generator uses distinct anchor colors and maps\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/18/code.py:57:#    - Trails are monochrome and use the sprite's own marker color, so they clearly read as source\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/18/code.py:81:    - Interior foreground components are sprites with a dominant body color and a rarer marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/18/code.py:85:    - Pair each sprite with the anchor whose color matches its marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/18/code.py:87:    - Corner anchors use the marker's larger current offset from the corner to choose the axis: larger column-gap means horizontal motion, otherwise vertical motion. After moving the sprite, repaint its original 4-neighbor boundary cells in the marker color and keep the original marker cell(s) instead of clearing the whole source site.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3e6067c3/5/code.py:14:#      input control row (left to right): `[####][#][###]`, where `#` is a fixed marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3e6067c3/5/code.py:17:#      Each bridge is still painted with the current frame's dot color, not with the control marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3e6067c3/5/code.py:31:#    - A single dedicated control-marker color is used for every run, so the task cannot collapse back into\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3e6067c3/5/code.py:79:#    - `main()` assumes the control row below all frames contains contiguous runs of one marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/30/code.py:71:#   - No new marker colors are introduced beyond the small background palette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/26/code.py:31:#   repeated marker colors would mostly create forced collisions. The generator therefore uses\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/26/code.py:41:#   generator can no longer rely on a free alignment axis or on repeated marker colors, so it\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/26/code.py:55:#   The new `main()` assumes the minority color inside each movable sprite is the marker color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/26/code.py:57:#   at most one anchor per marker color and that wrapped placements are collision-free; the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/26/code.py:290:    - Match every sprite to the border anchor that shares its marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/14/code.py:15:#     - The base task paints a fixed 4x4 glyph for each marker color and ignores marker position.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/14/code.py:27:#     - The generator always exposes both factors separately: all four marker colors appear inside\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/14/code.py:33:#     - The original solver assumed each marker color owned one final mask. That no longer holds;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/14/code.py:51:#     - The focus marker color appears in all four quadrants, so the learner can also see a single\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/14/code.py:66:#     - main() assumes isolated marker pixels are the only occurrences of marker colors outside the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/14/code.py:237:    - The final orientation is a composition of two visible factors: the marker color tells which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/27/code.py:17:#    - Original 581f7754 paired a sprite to an anchor by marker color, then stopped exactly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/27/code.py:29:#      marker ends at column 10. The old task would instead use marker colors and no extra\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/27/code.py:58:#    - Because marker colors never match anchor colors, the old color-based rule is visibly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/27/code.py:69:#    - They also differ from the source task because no sprite marker color equals any anchor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D3/1/code.py:36:#     - The marker color is fixed and excluded from the TL motif palette so the new cue is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/12/code.py:57:#    - Human solvers can see that marker colors live only on the border while output glyphs appear\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/12/code.py:74:#    - For any marker color, each of the four sides contributes at most one coordinate cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/12/code.py:266:    - Black background with marker colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8 appearing only on the outer border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/12/code.py:267:    - A top+left pair or a bottom+right pair of the same marker color encodes one glyph top-left coordinate.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dfadab01/12/code.py:271:    - Reconstruct each placement from its edge pair, then map the marker color to the same fixed glyph alphabet as the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/8/code.py:70:#    - Each sprite still uses only one body color plus one marker color, avoiding clutter despite the extra marker cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/8/code.py:100:    - Border anchors are isolated non-background singletons, and their colors match sprite marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/8/code.py:103:    - Pair each sprite with the anchor that shares its marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3dc255db/D4/1/code.py:86:# - No hidden color ordering is used. The marker color always matches the cup's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/24/code.py:22:#        1. match a sprite to the anchor sharing its minority marker color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/24/code.py:288:    - Pair each sprite with the anchor of the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/1/code.py:16:#       marker color inside a sprite selects an anchor of the same color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/1/code.py:17:#       several sprites may share one marker color and therefore one anchor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/1/code.py:30:#       even if the largest sprite's marker color is 6 and the first anchor color is 2,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/1/code.py:52:#     separation, so marker colors never match anchor colors at all.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/1/code.py:63:#   - Human solvers can see that there are as many anchors as sprites, that marker colors do\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/1/code.py:87:    - Each sprite has one minority-color marker pixel, but marker colors do not determine which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/3/code.py:39:    - marker color is fixed and reserved, so humans can read it as a symbol instead of as motif content\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/3/code.py:59:    - motif colors exclude the divider and marker colors to reduce clutter\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/3/code.py:570:    - Interior foreground components are mostly multi-color sprites whose least-frequent marker color matches a border anchor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/3/code.py:574:    - Pair each movable sprite with the anchor of the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/11/code.py:98:    - Match each sprite to the anchor sharing its marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/7/code.py:25:#        * former body pixels are repainted to the anchor/marker color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/7/code.py:66:#    - Input shows three cues at once: body color, minority marker color, and visible border\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/7/code.py:82:#    - Because body colors are chosen distinct from marker colors, every sprite exhibits a real\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/7/code.py:89:#    - It assumes each movable multi-color component has a single minority marker color matched\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/7/code.py:103:    - Interior sprites use one body color plus a rarer marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/7/code.py:107:    - Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/47/code.py:60:#       clean and every piece remains a separate 8-component once marker color is ignored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/47/code.py:69:#     - background is never a connector color and never the marker color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/47/code.py:214:        assert all(len(m) == 1 for m in marker_comps), \"marker color must appear only as singleton pixels\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/17/code.py:16:#    - Every used marker color now has at least one opposite-edge anchor pair:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/17/code.py:316:    - Each marker color has an opposite-edge border-anchor pair, and one color may have extra same-color anchors on the perpendicular border pair as well.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b9c3697/15/code.py:42:#      was a structure. That fails here because valid movers contain both red and a marker color,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b9c3697/15/code.py:69:#    - Every valid moving piece has exactly one non-red marker color, and that color is equal to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/15/code.py:21:#      1. match each sprite to the anchor of the same marker color;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/15/code.py:27:#         marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/15/code.py:35:#    - The trail color is exactly the marker color; this keeps the extra visual language small and legible.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/15/code.py:634:    - Match each sprite to the anchor of the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/15/code.py:637:    - On its turn, remove only that sprite, step toward the target line until the next step would leave the grid or hit any non-background cell, paste the sprite there, and paint the marker's traversed cells as a one-cell-wide trail in the marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/bf45cf4b/16/code.py:36:#    - \"control is monochrome\" is false: it now has body color + marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/2/code.py:16:#   Each anchor still matches sprites by marker color, but it no longer chooses a single slide\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/2/code.py:23:#   (1,2). Suppose a sprite with marker color 4 starts with its marker at (7,8).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/2/code.py:259:    - Isolated border singletons act as anchors, and a sprite is paired to the anchor with the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/16/code.py:29:#    The generator keeps 2-3 anchor colors and 4-5 sprites so marker colors repeat and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/16/code.py:54:#    A human looking at a few examples can spot that marker colors still match anchors,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/16/code.py:60:#    even when several sprites share a marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/16/code.py:82:    - Match each sprite to the anchor of the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/7/code.py:21:#    1. First infer the marker color as the unique least-frequent non-background\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/7/code.py:56:#    Across examples, the marker color changes and is not red, but is always the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/7/code.py:58:#    pixels of the same color remain, making both \"identify the marker color\" and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/7/code.py:222:    marker color, erase only true marker pixels, and keep same-color decoys.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/7/code.py:433:    - marker color is non-red and uniquely least frequent in the input,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/9/code.py:51:#       marker color as the handle that must reach the crossing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/9/code.py:123:    - Each moving sprite is a 2-color component whose minority marker color matches one center pixel; only that marker lies on the rail.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/28a6681f/D6/0/code.py:37:#    - Only one new symbol is introduced: marker color 8.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/28a6681f/D6/0/code.py:70:#    - The marker color is constant and structurally attached to the basin walls, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/28a6681f/D6/0/code.py:75:#      marker color. The marker reuses wall cells rather than floating in open air, so it\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/10/code.py:97:    - Match each multicolor sprite to the border anchor with the same marker color, then compute the one-axis translation that aligns the marker with that anchor row or column.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/21/code.py:19:#    frequent color is a marker. Each sprite slides toward the anchor of the same marker color,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/21/code.py:35:#    That keeps the hidden state small enough to infer while still making \"same marker color moves\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/21/code.py:59:#    settles second, while matching sprites of the same marker color move together.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/21/code.py:66:#    marker colors are used, reducing ambiguity about which group is earlier.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/21/code.py:73:#    main() assumes each marker color maps to exactly one anchor and that grouped clearing never\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/21/code.py:75:#    marker color and keeps input components non-overlapping.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/21/code.py:269:    - Match each movable component to the unique anchor of the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/21/code.py:271:    - Components sharing one marker color move together, different marker colors are processed in ascending color order, and each group's translated pixels overwrite whatever is currently present; later groups therefore cover earlier groups, while pixels pushed out of bounds are clipped.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:18:#    a sprite marker color 4 matched the unique border anchor color 4.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:22:#    marker colors rather than the anchor colors themselves.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:41:#    - Color equality no longer identifies the anchor; marker colors must be translated via legend.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:53:#    in one corner; the same anchor colors appear in both halves; and the sprite marker colors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:55:#    a human can see both that \"same marker color can live in both halves\" and that the border\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:59:#    Body colors avoid both backgrounds, the legend frame, and all semantic anchor/marker colors,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:75:    - One corner contains a framed legend whose inner rows encode [anchor color, marker color].\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:76:    - Outside the legend, isolated border pixels are anchors and interior sprites carry the marker colors from the legend.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:80:    - For each sprite, use its own region plus its marker color to identify the matching border anchor in that same region.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/19/code.py:83:    Key insight: Neither color cue is sufficient alone: the legend translates marker colors into anchor colors, and the repeated anchor colors are disambiguated by the sprite's background partition.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/4/code.py:19:#      sprites whose least-frequent color is the marker. Matching is still by marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/4/code.py:24:#        top-edge anchor color 4 at column 3, input marker color 4 at (5,6).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/4/code.py:31:#    - Use the marker color itself for the trail; this makes the correspondence visible without adding\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/4/code.py:64:#    - Because trail color equals marker color and each sample repeats colors across multiple sprites,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/4/code.py:98:    - Isolated non-background border singletons are anchors, and anchor colors match sprite marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/4/code.py:101:    - Pair each sprite with the anchor of the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/4/code.py:103:    - Instead of moving toward the anchor, the sprite moves the same distance away from it, then the cleared source marker path is redrawn as a continuous trail in the marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/37/code.py:19:#    - D9 changes \"what counts\" in the controls: only one echoed marker color per control matters.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/37/code.py:20:#      UL's marker color is the unique non-black color it shares with LL. UR's marker color is the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/37/code.py:31:#    - Each control quadrant has exactly one echoed marker color and at least one distractor pixel.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/37/code.py:279:    Infer the two control marker colors from palette echoes, then cross-map those counts into the output size.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/28/code.py:410:    - Match each movable sprite to the anchor with the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/13/code.py:57:#    - Original matching key was just marker color. That is no longer sufficient because two\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/29/code.py:33:#     - the set of border-marker colors is the \"allowed colors\" legend\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/23/code.py:65:    - Isolated non-background border pixels are anchors; each anchor color matches the marker color of the sprites it controls.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/23/code.py:68:    - Match each movable component to the anchor with the same marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/22/code.py:13:#   - Original task: irregular bodies with a side-attached marker were moved by matching marker color to a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/22/code.py:180:    - Each movable multicolor sprite is one 8-connected component made of a solid rectangle body plus one 1-pixel marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/22/code.py:184:    - Pair every sprite with the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/6/code.py:21:- Base task: a special marker color defines a row axis, a column axis, or a point\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/6/code.py:25:  2. Infer the marker color by counting all non-background cells in the whole\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/6/code.py:61:  2. The marker color is the unique least-frequent non-background color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/6/code.py:78:- Humans can see that the marker is not fixed red because the marker color varies\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7ed72f31/6/code.py:132:        raise ValueError(\"marker color is not uniquely least frequent\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/6/code.py:19:#    - Each interior sprite still has a large body color and a rarer marker color, but the marker is now a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/6/code.py:110:      marker color; the marker cells form a 2-3 cell straight segment.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/30/code.py:28:#    - Base task logic is preserved: each interior sprite has a rare marker color that matches one isolated\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/30/code.py:243:    - Match each movable sprite to the anchor sharing its marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db0c5428/D1/3/code.py:42:#      That assumption is removed; slot choice is now read from marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db0c5428/D1/3/code.py:190:    - For each outer slot, read its marker color and copy in the surrounding 3x3\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db0c5428/D1/3/code.py:194:    The marker colors create a color-to-block lookup, so destination slots must be\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db0c5428/D1/3/code.py:229:                    \"reused marker colors require identical source blocks\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/14/code.py:56:#      frame; same-looking small hollow sprites with unmatched marker colors do not move.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/14/code.py:81:#      winning marker color has no border anchor. The generator constructs exactly that.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/14/code.py:91:    - Extra small hollow sprites use the same pattern, but their topological marker color does not appear among the border anchors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/581f7754/12/code.py:69:    - Read each sprite's marker color from that topmost/leftmost pixel and match it to the secondary-ring anchor of the same color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/1/code.py:13:#      the hole's surrounding outline pixels into the same marker color. The copied color is still\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/1/code.py:14:#      the cavity's main fill color, but the correspondence is now \"marker color match\" rather\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/1/code.py:32:#      Here each component is \"majority fill color + one minority marker color\", so the solver\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/1/code.py:79:    Fill each `dst` hole with the main fill color of the `src` cavity whose marker color matches\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/25/code.py:34:#      the river. No extra marker colors are introduced.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/7/code.py:14:#         outline. Equal marker colors define the pairing, so geometry no longer decides\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/7/code.py:30:#      4 unique marker colors + 2 repeated src fill colors + 2 mapped legend colors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/7/code.py:52:#    - The marker colors each appear exactly twice across the input, once on src and once\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/7/code.py:76:#    - Each src cavity has exactly one unique marker color and one majority fill color;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/7/code.py:92:    Pair src cavities to dst holes by marker color, then recolor via a legend before filling.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/48/code.py:20:    不能 4 邻接。图例本身要从拼图碎片里剔除，避免污染面积、组件和 frame 检测。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/16/code.py:16:#         every source subsprite cell is rewritten to one uniform marker color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/16/code.py:27:#    - The marker color is a single reserved color for the whole sample, not a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/16/code.py:38:#      the absolute source fill pixels so it can rewrite them to the marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/16/code.py:66:#      become the same marker color. This is easy to spot from 2-3 samples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/16/code.py:70:#    - The grey marker color is absent from the input palette. Generator reserves color 5.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/16/code.py:335:    rewrite all src subsprite pixels to one marker color to show that the colors were\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/30/code.py:26:#        * one outside-marker color, visible on the tile border\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/30/code.py:27:#        * one inside-marker color, visible only inside a closed boundary\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/30/code.py:88:#    - Every generated sample must differ from the original overlay rule because both marker colors are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/30/code.py:97:#      one enclosed region of a single 