lors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c416de3/D1/2/code.py:43:#   that marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c416de3/D1/2/code.py:63:# - Colors still behave exactly as in the original: each target uses its own marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c416de3/D1/2/code.py:194:      sign flips, and paint it with the target corner's marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/31/code.py:332:    - Red and/or yellow marker colors are exactly the foreground colors whose 4-connected components are all singleton pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/31/code.py:350:    assert colors, \"expected foreground line and marker colors\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/7/code.py:43:#      both the body color and the direction-marker color, otherwise the new rule would\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/7/code.py:56:#    Only four colors are used at most: background, body/marker color, direction color,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/16/code.py:10:#     at the same destination, the highest marker color priority wins (7 > 3 > 1); after the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/16/code.py:15:#     6 because marker color 7 outranks 1, and both T shapes remain visible in colors 1 and 7.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/16/code.py:37:#     never reuse the same reserved marker color on one destination.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/16/code.py:399:    - If multiple T markers target the same destination, compare only their marker colors: 7 overrides 3, and 3 overrides 1.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/16/code.py:403:    - Geometry still tells you source versus destination, but now the marker color is itself part of the instruction, and semantics split into two layers: priority decides which repaint wins, while preservation means the control remains visible afterward.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b99e7126/