_combo/faa9f03d/8/code.py:250:    assert marker_colors, \"expected at least one marker color\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/8/code.py:260:        assert comps, \"marker color must have at least one component\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/21897d95/D8/2/code.py:18:#      new rule: two otherwise identical inputs both use the same \"┐\" shape; only the marker color flips\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/21897d95/D8/2/code.py:26:#      owned patch; the marker color is irrelevant there.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/21897d95/D8/2/code.py:31:#      region from sharing the marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/21897d95/D8/2/code.py:41:#    - No extra overlay is introduced; only the marker color changes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/24/code.py:18:#    The fused rule uses exactly three marker colors and exactly three logical\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/24/code.py:23:#    This is the D4/1 part: marker color no longer asks \"which nearby line is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/24/code.py:24:#    this for?\" because the marker color itself answers that question. The\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/24/code.py:25:#    D3/0 part is that the three marker colors still do three different jobs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/24/code.py:56:#        stay separate because its marker color identifies a different target\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/24/code.py:74:#      - the color legend is repeated in every sample because marker colors are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/24/code.py:269:    Repair a sparse rectilinear line network whose three singleton marker colors simultaneously encode role and target line color, including a forced 3-color crossing winner.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/24/code.py:272:    - The grid has a dominant background plus three logical line colors and up to three si