.py:71:   with markers present, that logic would wrongly count the marker color as a candidate wall color if not excluded.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/0/code.py:32:#      related to the current marker, while other marker colors belong to other\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/71e489b6/8/code.py:50:#    Only two output marker colors are introduced for a single structural cause:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/31f7f899/D11/1/code.py:76:# - There is exactly one marker color, and all its components are single pixels away from the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/31f7f899/D11/1/code.py:77:#   base row. The generator enforces this by fixing a dedicated grey marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:19:#   components in a separate marker color, each component being an exact copy of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:21:# - The same marker color also contains distractor components such as 2x2\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:36:# - To avoid an over-simplified \"consume everything of marker color\" rule, each\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:51:#   This variant needs a distinct marker color; otherwise exact-shape markers\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:66:# - Humans can see repeated copies of the sprite silhouette in the marker color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:74:# - Using one actor color and one marker color keeps the scene readable even\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:79:#   marker color always means static candidate shapes. Shape, not color id,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:233:    assert len(colors) == 2, f\"expected actor and marker colors, got {colors}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D1/2/code.py:338:    Key insight: The marker color contains multiple small shapes, but only rotated copies of the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ce