shi/code/9aaea919/D13/1/code.py:66:    Marker bars are thin 4-connected components of the given marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D7/2/code.py:79:    Marker bars are thin 4-connected components of the given marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D13/2/code.py:76:    Marker bars are thin 4-connected components of the given marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D6/0/code.py:72:    Marker bars are thin 4-connected components of the given marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/332f06d7/D3/2/code.py:86:#     - no extra marker color is introduced, avoiding visual clutter\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D5/0/code.py:179:    - Within each region, sort marker colors by boundary depth to form the palette, prepending the base color when the shallowest marker is not on the boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D5/0/code.py:249:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D6/1/code.py:48:    Marker bars are thin 4-connected components of the given marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D5/1/code.py:59:#       board with many unrelated marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D5/2/code.py:23:#     The original task uses each marker color as the paint assigned to its own\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D5/2/code.py:52:#     - Palette construction assumes at most one meaningful marker color per\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D5/2/code.py:180:    - Read the marker colors in increasing boundary-distance order to assemble the same cyclic palette as in the original task, prepending the base color when the first marker is not on the boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D5/2/code.py:210:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b0280bc/D6/3/code.py:9:#     - source: exactly 1 solid NxN square, fixed input marker color teal(8).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b0280bc/D6/3/code.py:10:#     - goal: exactly 1 solid NxN square, fixed input marker color maroon(9).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7b0280bc/D6/3/code.py:32:#     - Source/goal marker colors are fixed across all samples, so the role mapping\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D6/2/code.py:99:    Marker bars are thin 4-connected components of the given marker color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D9/0/code.py:92:    - each example uses 2-4 marker colors with repeated rows/columns so the priority is inferable\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D9/0/code.py:109:    - solver assumes marker colors belong to PROCESS_ORDER; generator samples only from that set\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D9/1/code.py:250:    - First, solve each region exactly like the original task: sort marker colors by boundary depth, prepend the base color if depth 0 is missing, and fill the region with palette[depth mod palette_length].\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D9/1/code.py:279:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9aaea919/D10/0/code.py:52:    \"\"\"Marker bars are thin 4-connected components of the given marker color.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D9/0/code.py:40:      - geometry, projectile distance, and bg marker colors stay unchanged, so D9 remains the only conceptual novelty\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D9/2/code.py:15:#     - If that lower-right corner is painted in a different marker color, that cell becomes\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D9/2/code.py:18:#       2/5/8 also tint their upper-left filled cell with their own marker color. This is the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D9/2/code.py:253:    - If a placed glyph's lower-right corner is painted in a different marker color, treat that corner cell as a new marker in the next wave.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/0/code.py:81:      field and at most one marker color per distance.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/0/code.py:203:    - For each region, sort the marker colors by boundary depth to recover the palette order, prepending the base color if the nearest marker is not on the boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/0/code.py:230:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D12/2/code.py:143:    Expand each real marker pixel into its fixed 4x4 glyph while later task examples simply enable more marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D12/2/code.py:302:    train uses 2 then 3 marker colors, and test uses 4.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D12/2/code.py:332:    Return the held-out test input from a task whose example sequence uses 2, then 3, then 4 marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D8/1/code.py:51:#     - bar_mask 只看 {blue, red}: 变体后必须扩展为 {all marker colors, red}。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D8/1/code.py:102:    Extend a green path across the grid while red obstacle bars advertise their keep-out radius by marker color instead of marker count.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D8/1/code.py:110:    - Read each bar's radius from its unique marker color: yellow->1, blue->2, pink->3, orange->4.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D8/1/code.py:149:        assert len(marker_colors) == 1, \"each obstacle bar must use exactly one marker color\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D8/1/code.py:151:        assert marker_color in MARKER_TO_RADIUS, \"unknown obstacle marker color\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/2/code.py:25:#    - Only two marker colors are used, so the symbolic mapping is learnable from 2-4 examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/2/code.py:26:#    - Each grid contains both marker colors and at least one marker/topology mismatch, so the new\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/2/code.py:138:    - The local marker color encodes which branch to use; the solver must decode that symbol rather\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D2/1/code.py:157:            assert len(matches) == 1, f\"ambiguous legend for marker color {marker_color} at {(r_i, c_i)}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D2/1/code.py:177:    - First decode each legend: the label marker color tells which marker type owns the sample glyph immediately up-left of it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D2/1/code.py:190:    assert present_marker_colors, \"expected at least one marker color\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D2/1/code.py:192:        assert marker_color in mapping, f\"missing legend for marker color {marker_color}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/1/code.py:156:    - For each region, sort marker colors by boundary depth; if the shallowest marker is not on the boundary, insert the base color at the front.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/1/code.py:159:    Key insight: Marker depth still defines the palette order, but the trajectory is no longer periodic; the innermost marker color absorbs the entire remaining core instead of restarting the palette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/1/code.py:185:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/409aa875/D19/0/code.py:50:#   Only three output marker colors carry meaning: grey=safe shooter, maroon=hit sprite or lone bg\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D12/0/code.py:227:    - For every non-wall region, sort marker colors by boundary depth; if no depth-0 marker exists, prepend the region base color, then paint cyclic concentric frames with that palette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D12/0/code.py:258:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D12/0/code.py:373:      - used: set of marker colors used\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D4/1/code.py:117:    - For each marker color, read the 4x4 sample just above-left of its legend label to learn that marker's glyph mask.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D6/1/code.py:151:    - The marker colors still select from a fixed glyph alphabet; the only new behavior is that glyph coordinates live on a wraparound surface rather than a clipped rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/2/code.py:49:#       - Use only 0-3 markers per region and 3-9 distinct marker colors grid-wide, which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/2/code.py:148:    - For each region, read marker colors ordered by their Chebyshev distance from the boundary, but do not build a repeating palette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/2/code.py:149:    - A marker colors only the full layer at its own distance, and only when that distance is even; odd layers and unmarked even layers stay at the region base color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D2/2/code.py:211:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D2/3/code.py:215:    - Keep the original 4x4 shape dictionary fixed for each marker color, and still place each glyph with its top-left corner at the marker location, clipping at the border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D6/3/code.py:176:    - Map each marker color to the same fixed glyph alphabet as the original puzzle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D4/2/code.py:23:#     - The glyph shape must therefore remain fixed per marker color; only the output color is instance-specific.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D4/2/code.py:29:#     - Output colors are chosen as a permutation/subset of {1,4,6,7}, not fixed to the original per-marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D4/2/code.py:46:#     - Assumption: there is exactly one contiguous left/right edge strip whose first column contains unique marker colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D4/0/code.py:52:inside the owning rectangle, on background cells, with distinct marker colors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D4/0/code.py:193:    - Read marker colors in boundary-distance order to build the same cyclic palette as in the original task, prepending the base color when the first marker is not on the boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D4/0/code.py:221:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D8/1/code.py:201:    - The drawn color is not fixed: find the nearest other real marker by Manhattan distance, then convert that neighbor's marker color through the original palette mapping 2->4, 3->1, 5->6, 8->7.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D8/3/code.py:33:#      5x7 region (area 35, max depth 2) with marker color R at depth 1:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D8/3/code.py:60:#      least one marker color across small and large regions. Humans can therefore\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D8/3/code.py:180:    - For each region, sort marker colors by Chebyshev distance from the boundary; if the shallowest marker is not on the boundary, prepend the base color as before.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D8/3/code.py:233:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D17/0/code.py:14:#     Azure(8) is background, blue(1)+red(2) form 1-cell-thick bars attached to alternating\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D10/1/code.py:85:#     colors 1/4/6/7, which are disjoint from marker colors 2/3/5/8.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/1/code.py:63:#    - Blue remains the dominant non-marker color; generator keeps enough blue mass for that.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D4/1/code.py:64:#    - Port color is still the unique non-marker color 4-adjacent to blue; generator preserves\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D1/1/code.py:126:#    - No new color remapping is introduced: marker colors still map to the same output colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D8/2/code.py:8:#   marker color still chooses which mask to stamp. The change is only the paint\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D8/2/code.py:14:#   been color 4 because only the marker color mattered.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D8/2/code.py:170:    - The marker color still selects the glyph mask: 2=frame, 3=ring, 5=diagonal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D4/1/code.py:66:#    Colors keep their original role: marker colors define the repeating palette by\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D4/1/code.py:252:    - For each region, sort the singleton marker colors by Chebyshev distance from that region's outer boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D4/1/code.py:286:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D8/2/code.py:28:#    marker-bearing regions and uses globally unique marker colors, so the global\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D8/2/code.py:36:#    collect marker colors globally before processing region fill.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D8/2/code.py:124:    - Gather all marker colors across the entire grid in row-major order, keeping the first occurrence of each color to form one global palette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D8/2/code.py:225:#   globally distinct marker colors to keep that order unambiguous and visible.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D2/2/code.py:87:    - Azure background with blue diamond/octagon blobs; green edge pixels mark each blob's outgoing port side.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D3/1/code.py:19:#    - The glyph dictionary itself is unchanged: marker color still chooses one fixed 4x4 boolean mask and one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D3/1/code.py:53:#    - Solver assumptions are matched by construction: for each marker color there is at most one top, bottom,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D3/1/code.py:57:#    - Across examples, marker colors are absent from the interior and appear only on the border. Human solvers can\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D3/1/code.py:196:    - Black background with marker colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8 appearing only on the outer border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D3/1/code.py:197:    - A top+left pair or bottom+right pair of the same marker color encodes one glyph top-left coordinate.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D7/0/code.py:124:    - Treat each 4-connected non-wall component independently and order its marker colors by Chebyshev distance from that component's own boundary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D10/0/code.py:177:    - For every region, compute each marker's 4-neighbor (Manhattan) distance to the region boundary and order marker colors by that depth.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5961cc34/D17/2/code.py:134:    # --- STEP 2) Identify the blob body color and the port-marker color ---\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D3/2/code.py:47:#      {1,4,6,7}, which are disjoint from marker colors {2,3,5,8}, so role confusion is limited.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dfadab01/D3/2/code.py:49:#    - No new color logic is introduced. The fixed mapping from marker color to glyph color/pattern\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D7/3/code.py:171:    - Within a region, sort marker colors by Chebyshev distance from the region boundary; if the first marker is not on the boundary, prepend the region's background color, then repaint the region with palette[distance mod palette_length].\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D7/3/code.py:233:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D10/1/code.py:168:    - For each region, sort marker colors by the integer part of their Euclidean distance from the exterior; prepen